MENTOR: We're focused on **Title** now.

**What's happening on Title right now:** between **0.5 s and 1.5 s**, Title's Position, Scale and Opacity all change at a **constant speed**. Then at 1.5 s Position keeps going at a new constant speed until **3 s**, and stops dead. That's what linear means: no speeding up and no slowing down. Each key starts or stops abruptly, which feels mechanical.

I pulled a frame at **1 s**, the middle of those first pairs, where Title is moving at full speed. It came back as a plain purple swatch, though, so I can't point to Title in it. Scrub to 1 s in your own comp and watch Title there. With linear keys, the speed at 1 s is the same as the speed just after 0.5 s.

**Easing** fixes this. The layer slows as it comes into a key and speeds up as it leaves one, so the motion settles instead of slamming to a stop.

**How to do it:**
1. In the timeline, select the keys you want to ease.
2. Go to **Animation › Keyframe Assistant › Easy Ease**, or press **F9**.
3. The diamond keys turn into hourglass shapes.
4. To check, open the **Graph Editor** (the graph icon above the layers) and switch to the Speed Graph. Linear shows flat lines with sudden jumps. Eased shows smooth hills.

**Your turn.** Ease these 4 pairs on Title:
- **Position** 0.5 s → 1.5 s
- **Position** 1.5 s → 3 s
- **Scale** 0.5 s → 1.5 s
- **Opacity** 0.5 s → 1.5 s

Tip: press **U** with Title selected to show only its keyframed properties.

Say **"done"** when you're ready and I'll check. If you get stuck, ask and I'll show you one pair.

